as only one diagonal open 3.\nSo G7 seems stronger! It covers more ground.\n\nHow should I proceed?\n\nIf I play G7, White must block it.\nIf I play I8, the diagonal is open.\nI8 is close to the stones H8, H7, G6 but it has no overlap.\n\nShould I think broader?\n\nIf I play G7, my vertical open 3 makes it strong.\nG8 or G4 are the choices.\n\nIf I play I8, my diagonal is good but not strong.\n\nThe key intersection is **G7**. It\'s the point where multiple lines have the possibility to join up, especially after the White block.\n\nG7 gives me a very good vertical open 3 and diagonal blocked 3.\nBecause it links to the current diagonal.\n\nSo I am confirming **G7 (7,7)**. It also forms a strategic position.\n\nAfter a thorough board check for the move, G7, it\'s perfect. G7 is empty and I am confident in this decision.\n\n**My final analysis**:\nMy analysis of the board is that playing G7 strategically makes the best move. It covers two possible connections (vertical and diagonal). It also forces White into action.\n\nJSON Coordinate {"col": "G", "row": 7}\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: The current board shows Black (X) building momentum in the center and lower-right quadrant. White (O) has a few scattered stones but no immediate lethal threats (no 4-in-a-row or open-3 that can\'t be countered). Black has a cluster of stones around G5, G6, H7, H8, and I9.\n\nThe most promising move is **G7**.\n1.  **Intersection of Lines:** G7 lies at the intersection of two Black lines:\n    *   **Vertical Line:** It connects with G5 and G6 to form a vertical line of 3 stones (G5-G6-G7). This is an **Open 3** because both ends (G4 and G8) are empty and unblocked.\n    *   **Diagonal Line:** It connects with H8 and I9 to form a diagonal line of 3 stones (G7-H8-I9). While this line is blocked on one end by White\'s stone at F6, the other end at J10 is open. This creates a secondary threat ("Blocked 3" or "Sleeping 3").\n\nReason: G7 is a powerful multi-purpose move. By creating a ver